    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d77">

                <pb facs="0071" n="67" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg67"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/12306: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Hankow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-02-04T09:00:00+08:00">February 4, 1938—9
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 4—7 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>79. In recent conversation with Vice Minister Hsu Mo latter informed Peck<anchor
                        n="98" xml:id="d77-f1"/> that the Secretary of State had said to the Chinese
                    Ambassador in regard to reports that Great Britain, France and the Soviet Union
                    were discussing plan for assisting China to resist Japan, that the United States
                    would go as far as other powers in aiding China but declined to commit itself in
                    regard to any plan still in stage of discussion. The remark of the Vice Minister
                    was made casually in a general conversation but may be of interest.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d77-f1" n="98" xml:id="d77-f1-content">
                <p>Willys R. Peck, Counselor of Embassy in China.</p>
            </note>
